For any real x, write x = n + t with n = \lfloor x floor and t = \{x\} \in [0,1). Then \lceil x ceil equals n if t=0 (x integer), and n+1 if t \in (0,1). Thus

\lceil x ceil - x = egin{cases} 0, & t=0,\ 1 - t, & t\in(0,1). \end{cases}

Hence \lceil x ceil - x \in [0,1), with 0 attained at integers in the interval and values arbitrarily close to 1 from below but never reaching 1. Therefore the range is [0,1).

For a reflection across a plane with unit normal \(\mathbf{u}\), the matrix is \(R = I - 2\mathbf{u}\mathbf{u}^T\). Thus \(\dfrac{I - R}{2} = \mathbf{u}\mathbf{u}^T\), a rank-1 matrix whose columns are multiples of \(\mathbf{u}\).
Compute \(S = \dfrac{I - R}{2}\):
\[I - R = \begin{pmatrix}
1 - \tfrac{7}{9} & 0 - \tfrac{4}{9} & 0 - (-\tfrac{4}{9}) \\
0 - \tfrac{4}{9} & 1 - \tfrac{1}{9} & 0 - \tfrac{8}{9} \\
0 - (-\tfrac{4}{9}) & 0 - \tfrac{8}{9} & 1 - \tfrac{1}{9}
\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}
\tfrac{2}{9} & -\tfrac{4}{9} & \tfrac{4}{9} \\
-\tfrac{4}{9} & \tfrac{8}{9} & -\tfrac{8}{9} \\
\tfrac{4}{9} & -\tfrac{8}{9} & \tfrac{8}{9}
\end{pmatrix}.\]
So
\[S = \frac{I-R}{2} = \begin{pmatrix}
\tfrac{1}{9} & -\tfrac{2}{9} & \tfrac{2}{9} \\
-\tfrac{2}{9} & \tfrac{4}{9} & -\tfrac{4}{9} \\
\tfrac{2}{9} & -\tfrac{4}{9} & \tfrac{4}{9}
\end{pmatrix}.\]
Any nonzero column is proportional to the unit normal. Taking the first column gives a direction \(\propto (1,-2,2)\). The primitive integer normal with \(a>0\) is \(\begin{pmatrix}1\\-2\\2\end{pmatrix}\).

For reflections, \(R = I - 2\mathbf{u}\mathbf{u}^T\). Hence \(S = \dfrac{I - R}{2} = \mathbf{u}\mathbf{u}^T\).
Compute \(I - R\):
\[I - R = \begin{pmatrix}
1 - (-\tfrac{7}{11}) & 0 - (-\tfrac{6}{11}) & 0 - (-\tfrac{6}{11}) \\
0 - (-\tfrac{6}{11}) & 1 - \tfrac{9}{11} & 0 - (-\tfrac{2}{11}) \\
0 - (-\tfrac{6}{11}) & 0 - (-\tfrac{2}{11}) & 1 - \tfrac{9}{11}
\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}
\tfrac{18}{11} & \tfrac{6}{11} & \tfrac{6}{11} \\
\tfrac{6}{11} & \tfrac{2}{11} & \tfrac{2}{11} \\
\tfrac{6}{11} & \tfrac{2}{11} & \tfrac{2}{11}
\end{pmatrix}.\]
Thus
\[S = \frac{I-R}{2} = \begin{pmatrix}
\tfrac{9}{11} & \tfrac{3}{11} & \tfrac{3}{11} \\
\tfrac{3}{11} & \tfrac{1}{11} & \tfrac{1}{11} \\
\tfrac{3}{11} & \tfrac{1}{11} & \tfrac{1}{11}
\end{pmatrix}.\]
A nonzero column is proportional to the unit normal; the first column is \(\propto (9,3,3)\), which reduces to \((3,1,1)\). With \(a>0\), the primitive integer normal is \(\begin{pmatrix}3\\1\\1\end{pmatrix}\).

For a reflection matrix \(R\), we have \(R = I - 2\mathbf{u}\mathbf{u}^T\). Then \(S = \dfrac{I - R}{2} = \mathbf{u}\mathbf{u}^T\).
Compute \(I - R\):
\[I - R = \begin{pmatrix}
1 - \tfrac{1}{9} & 0 - \tfrac{4}{9} & 0 - (-\tfrac{8}{9}) \\
0 - \tfrac{4}{9} & 1 - \tfrac{7}{9} & 0 - \tfrac{4}{9} \\
0 - (-\tfrac{8}{9}) & 0 - \tfrac{4}{9} & 1 - \tfrac{1}{9}
\end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix}
\tfrac{8}{9} & -\tfrac{4}{9} & \tfrac{8}{9} \\
-\tfrac{4}{9} & \tfrac{2}{9} & -\tfrac{4}{9} \\
\tfrac{8}{9} & -\tfrac{4}{9} & \tfrac{8}{9}
\end{pmatrix}.\]
Thus
\[S = \frac{I-R}{2} = \begin{pmatrix}
\tfrac{4}{9} & -\tfrac{2}{9} & \tfrac{4}{9} \\
-\tfrac{2}{9} & \tfrac{1}{9} & -\tfrac{2}{9} \\
\tfrac{4}{9} & -\tfrac{2}{9} & \tfrac{4}{9}
\end{pmatrix}.\]
Taking the first column gives a direction proportional to \((4,-2,4)\), which simplifies to \((2,-1,2)\). With \(a>0\), the primitive integer normal is \(\begin{pmatrix}2\\-1\\2\end{pmatrix}\).
